# escape_shade_laoch -- the old warrior, Ferchar of Sliabh Fuait, one of the dead waiting at the well. A hundred years
# making the speech he will give Géaga. Before she looks away he waits as you do; after, she has let you go.

@file underworld/escape
@encounter 9

= the speech
@when !note escape_geaga_blind_eye
@continue
A hundred years I have been fashioning what I will say to her. The words are good now. Well-made.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
I was never a man of poems. The poets came to me, and I gave them silver.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
Hear it, and judge it.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]

= as she will hear it
@when !note escape_geaga_blind_eye
@continue
Géaga of the well, of the nine hazels and the nine gifts: I was Ferchar of Sliabh Fuait.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
Three kings called me champion. At the ford of the Bóinn I held alone,
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
and what was left of me after was still standing. Give me one morning above, one fight in the open air.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]

= the ending
@when !note escape_geaga_blind_eye
Is that well enough? The middle part I have changed four times.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
A hundred years, and the ending is still not right.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]

= she let you go
@when note escape_geaga_blind_eye
@continue
She let you go. She looked at you, and she let you go.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
A hundred years, and she has not once looked at me long enough to hear it.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
Well. It is no matter. You are going up, and I am not, and that is the shape of things.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]

= tell them he held
@when note escape_geaga_blind_eye
Find someone of my blood, if any are left above. Tell them Ferchar of Sliabh Fuait held.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
In every place they set him, he held, and he did not fall.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
Tell them nothing else. That is enough. That is more than enough.
% [Gaeilge le teacht]
